# Git safety

- Never commit directly to the base or production branch (see `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.md` for
  which branches those are in this repo). Always work on a branch prefixed per
  `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.md` (default allowlist `agent/,claude/,codex/` — `agent/` is what the
  kit's own scripts create; keep a hosted platform's pre-assigned `claude/`/`codex/` branch as-is).
- Never create a branch/PR that targets the production branch directly, or push/merge the base
  branch into it, without the human explicitly saying `approve main` (separate from plain
  `approve`) and the PR body carrying the required authorization marker — see `AGENTS.md` →
  "Production-branch gate (`approve main`)".
- Stage explicit files (`git add <file> <file>`). Never `git add -A` or `git add .` — you must
  know exactly what you're committing.
- Never force-push a branch another agent or human might also be working on.
- Never amend or rewrite history on a branch that may already be pulled elsewhere.
- Never merge a pull request, never mark one ready for review, never add or remove the
  `no-automerge` label — open it as a draft; the human marks it ready, and `auto-merge.yml`
  merges it once its checks are green (`docs/ai/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md` → "Auto-merge after green").
- Never run destructive git commands (`reset --hard`, `clean -f`, `branch -D`, force-push to a
  shared branch) without explicit human instruction for that specific action.
- Never commit secrets, tokens, or `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.env`.
